  {"question": "According to arXiv 2302.07103, how does the definition of validation for ML models differ from that for medical devices, and what is the role of clinical evaluation?", "answer": "ML validation focuses on unbiased model evaluation and parameter tuning, while medical device validation aims to demonstrate clinical benefit and intended use, with clinical evaluation as a key part.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "Validation of machine learning (ML) models is technically understood to be an approach to algorithm tuning [9]. Validation offers an approach for choosing algorithm learning parameters, so-called hyperparameters, such that the subsequently trained model is less likely to overfit the training data."}, {"page": 14, "quote": "The goal of the validation is to demonstrate clinical benefit, and achievement of intended use, alongside appropriate safety and performance. Validation, then, is built upon clinical evaluations 1 and investigations and a summative usability evaluation."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2302.07103, what are the main differences between validation activities for pharmaceutical manufacturing automation and for software used in drug development?", "answer": "Manufacturing automation validation focuses on the entire system in its environment, using protocols like IQ, OQ, PQ, while drug development validation emphasizes regulatory compliance and risk-based confirmation for software driving clinical processes.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "Particularly important protocols to be followed in manufacturing automation are installation, operational and performance qualification (IQ, OQ and PQ respectively)."}, {"page": 30, "quote": "Entire software system used to drive a clinical development process including referenced databases. See section, 'Drug development (R&D).'" }]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2302.07103, what does IEC 62366-1 require for usability engineering in medical devices?", "answer": "IEC 62366-1 requires a usability engineering process that addresses safety issues and hazard-related use scenarios, with formative and summative evaluations to ensure product usability.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "IEC 62366-1 (Medical Devices - Part 1: Application of usability engineering to medical devices) [15] defines a set of requirements a usability engineering process must fulfill in order to achieve product usability. In particular, it focuses on potential safety issues and hazard-related use scenarios. In order to achieve a quality user interface (UI), the UI is first specified and then undergoes both formative and summative evaluations."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2302.07103, what is meant by 'narrow validation' and how is it related to 'broad validation'?", "answer": "Narrow validation is a subset of broad validation, focusing on confirming intended use or application, while broad validation encompasses all best-practices from quality management.", "evidence": [{"page": 26, "quote": "To bring clarity, we describe broad validation as the all-encompassing validation best-practices stemming from quality management activities. Whereas, narrow validation involves only a subset of these activities."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2302.07103, what is the main goal of validation for in-vitro diagnostic devices?", "answer": "The main goal is to confirm safety, performance, and clinical benefit, including clinical performance, through clinical evaluation and investigations.", "evidence": [{"page": 30, "quote": "Confirmation of safety, performance and clinical benefit of the product (narrow validation). For IVDs this validation includes clinical performance,"}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2302.07103, what principle must data used for drug licensing in pharmaceutical R&D follow?", "answer": "Data must follow ALCOA principles: Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous, Original, and Accurate.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "Good data and record management practices (GDRP) combined with a requirement that data used, to license a drug for market, follow ALCOA principles - Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous, Original, and Accurate - oblige software used to be developed following engineering best practices [20, 21]."}]}
]}
